## Why This Is Asked

This question tests real-world patterns: per-field state via closures, per-field debouncing, cancellation of outdated requests with AbortController, and a global concurrency limiter to prevent overload, plus mapping responses to correct field.

## Key Concepts

- Closures for per-field state
- Debounce patterns
- AbortController cancellation
- Concurrency limit with a simple semaphore
- Mapping async results to UI per-field

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal concurrency-limited debounced fetch per field using closures
function createFieldController(fetchUrl, maxConcurrent = 4) {
  let debounceTimer;
  let abortController;

  return {
    onInput: async (term) => {
      // Cancel previous request
      if (abortController) {
        abortController.abort();
      }

      // Clear existing debounce
      clearTimeout(debounceTimer);

      // Set new debounce
      debounceTimer = setTimeout(async () => {
        // Check global concurrency limit
        if (globalInFlight >= maxConcurrent) {
          await waitForSlot();
        }

        globalInFlight++;
        abortController = new AbortController();

        try {
          const response = await fetch(`${fetchUrl}?q=${term}`, {
            signal: abortController.signal
          });

          if (!abortController.signal.aborted) {
            const suggestions = await response.json();
            updateFieldUI(suggestions);
          }
        } catch (error) {
          if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
            console.error('Fetch error:', error);
          }
        } finally {
          globalInFlight--;
        }
      }, 300);
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a streaming, per-tenant UI with time-based playback, memory controls, and precise cleanup when tenants switch. It exercises useSyncExternalStore, memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow), bounded ring buffers to cap memory, React.memo for EventRow components, playback controls (play/pause and seek), and tenant switch cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- useSyncExternalStore for external streams
- memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
- Bounded ring buffer to cap memory
- React.memo for EventRow components
- Playback controls: play/pause and seek
- Tenant switch cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Stream wrapper using useSyncExternalStore
function useAuditStream(tenantId) {
  const storeRef = useRef(null);
  if (!storeRef.current) {
    storeRef.current = createAuditStore(tenantId);
  }
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    storeRef.current.subscribe,
    storeRef.current.getSnapshot,
    storeRef.current.getServerSnapshot
  );
}

// Memoized selector keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
const selectBoundedEvents = memoize((tenantId, timeWindow) => {
  return (events) => {
    const cutoff = Date.now() - timeWindow;
    return events
      .filter(e => e.tenantId === tenantId && e.timestamp > cutoff)
      .slice(-120); // Bounded to 120 events
  };
});

// React.memo EventRow
const EventRow = memo(({ event }) => (
  <div>{event.action} - {event.timestamp}</div>
));

// Panel wiring
function AuditPanel({ tenantId }) {
  const events = useAuditStream(tenantId);
  const boundedEvents = useMemo(
    () => selectBoundedEvents(tenantId, 120000),
    [tenantId]
  )(events);

  return (
    <div>
      {boundedEvents.map(e => <EventRow key={e.id} event={e} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

